==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|iniquite|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|iniquité|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|iniquitas|lang=la}}, from {{term|iniquus|lang=la||unjust, harmful}}, from {{m|la|in-}} + {{m|la|aequus||equal}}. Compare [[inequity]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɪnˈɪk.wɪ.ti/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|iniquities}}

# [[deviation|Deviation]] from what is right; [[wickedness]], [[gross]] [[injustice]].
#* '''1994''', [[w:Pulp Fiction (film)#Jules.27s_Bible_passage|Jules, ''Pulp Fiction'']],
#*: The path of the righteous man is beset on all sides by the '''iniquities''' of the selfish and the tyranny of evil men.
#* {{quote-news|author=Oliver Brown|title=Oscar Pistorius jailed for five years – sport afforded no protection against his tragic fallibilities: Bladerunner's punishment for killing Reeva Steenkamp is but a frippery when set against the burden that her bereft parents, June and Barry, must carry &#91;print version: No room for sentimentality in this tragedy, 13 September 2014, p.&nbsp;S22&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/oscar-pistorius/11089621/Oscar-Pistorius-jailed-for-five-years-sport-afforded-no-protection-against-his-tragic-fallibilities.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Sport)|date=21 October 2014|passage=But ever since the concept of "hamartia" recurred through {{w|Aristotle}}'s ''[[w:Poetics (Aristotle)|Poetics]]'', in an attempt to describe man's ingrained '''iniquity''', our impulse has been to identify a telling defect in those brought suddenly and dramatically low.}}
# A [[wrongful]] [[act]].
# [[absence|Absence]] of [[moral]] or [[spiritual]] [[values]], [[lawlessness]].
# [[denial|Denial]] of the [[sovereignty]] of [[God]].

{{rfex}}

